<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  NOT LOADED. Adopt it at a natural break between games:

      cp ~/civvis-game-supervisor.launchd.plist \
         ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.civvis.game-supervisor.plist
      launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) \
         ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.civvis.game-supervisor.plist

  and stop the `&`-launched supervisor in civvis-interactive-host.sh, or the two
  will both drive the one Civ 6 install (gamelock.py will refuse the second, but
  that is a collision to avoid rather than to rely on).

  WHY: `civvis-interactive-host.sh` starts the supervisor with `&`, and macOS
  puts a shell-backgrounded process in a lower priority band. Measured
  2026-08-11 on this machine, the identical command:

      foreground : nice 0
      backgrounded (`&`) : nice 5      <- whole subtree inherits it

  So civ6_play.py and Civ6_Exe_Child run at nice 5 while ordinary work -- a
  199% rustc, martbot jobs, Chrome -- sits at nice 0 ABOVE the one process the
  whole ladder depends on. It cannot be repaired after the fact: macOS lets an
  unprivileged process raise its nice but never lower it, so a game that starts
  demoted stays demoted for its whole run.

  This is the same defect com.civvis.keeper.plist was written to fix ("the one
  CIVVIS process a human actually watches was tied for last"), recurring on the
  path that actually drives the ladder. A launchd job with an explicit
  ProcessType and Nice is the remedy, verified here: the same script under
  ProcessType Interactive + Nice 0 comes up at nice 0.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>Label</key>
	<string>com.civvis.game-supervisor</string>

	<key>ProgramArguments</key>
	<array>
		<string>/usr/bin/caffeinate</string>
		<string>-dims</string>
		<string>/bin/zsh</string>
		<string>/Users/martin/civvis-game-supervisor.sh</string>
	</array>

	<key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
	<dict>
		<key>PATH</key>
		<string>/Users/martin/.cargo/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
		<key>HOME</key>
		<string>/Users/martin</string>
	</dict>

	<key>RunAtLoad</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- The supervisor is meant to be up whenever the machine is; the host script
	     already restarts it on exit, and this replaces that loop. -->
	<key>KeepAlive</key>
	<dict>
		<key>SuccessfulExit</key>
		<false/>
	</dict>
	<key>ThrottleInterval</key>
	<integer>60</integer>

	<key>WorkingDirectory</key>
	<string>/Users/martin</string>

	<key>StandardOutPath</key>
	<string>/Users/martin/civvis-game-supervisor.interactive.log</string>
	<key>StandardErrorPath</key>
	<string>/Users/martin/civvis-game-supervisor.interactive.log</string>

	<!-- ⚠ THE WHOLE POINT. Without an explicit ProcessType launchd treats the job
	     as Adaptive and demotes it, which is the same way the exhibition
	     supervisor ended up at nice 5. Civ 6's turn loop runs off frames, so
	     holding the game down does not merely slow the ladder, it changes what
	     the ladder measures. Declare it Interactive and pin nice 0. -->
	<key>ProcessType</key>
	<string>Interactive</string>
	<key>Nice</key>
	<integer>0</integer>
</dict>
</plist>
